    "created_at": "2016-05-31T06:05:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-05-31T06:05:27Z",
    "body": "On further reflection, I _think_ the situation is that imageSmoothingQuality + imageSmoothingEnabled together give a value set of \"pixelated\" (imageSmoothingEnabled = false), plus \"low\", \"medium\", and \"high\" (imageSmoothingEnabled = true). That was roughly @xidachen's original proposal (\"pixelated\", \"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"; no boolean) before I asked him to converge on the CSS names.\n\nI'm not sure what the CSS names map to though, besides that there's a \"pixelated\" and a \"high-quality\". \"crisp-edges\" seems unlike anything else. The \"auto\" vs. \"high-quality\" distinction is quite subtle and I'm unclear whether UAs actually plan to dynamically change the meaning of the keywords based on available system resources. I can't find a counterpart for \"medium\" or \"low\".\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
